course: history
lesson: 36
title: Computers and the internet
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In the 1840s Ada Lovelace wrote what many call the first computer program, for a calculating machine that Charles Babbage designed but never finished, and she imagined that one day machines might even make music.
explain: The first electronic computers of the 1940s filled whole rooms and were used to break codes and do huge sums; at Bletchley Park in Britain, Alan Turing helped break secret German codes in the Second World War.
explain: The microchip packed many tiny parts onto a small piece of silicon, which is why the phone in your pocket is millions of times more powerful than those room-sized machines.
explain: The internet began in 1969 by linking a few American university computers, and in 1989 Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web of pages and links and gave it to the world for free.
explain: Games grew from Pong's two bats and a dot in 1972 to Europa Universalis, but history games simplify the past, and online, just like a historian, you must check your sources.
fact: In the 1840s Ada Lovelace wrote what many call the first computer program, for Charles Babbage's planned calculating machine, which was never finished.
fact: Ada Lovelace imagined that computers could one day do more than numbers, even make music.
fact: The first electronic computers, in the 1940s, filled whole rooms and were used to break codes and do huge calculations.
fact: Alan Turing helped break German codes at Bletchley Park in the Second World War and described how computers could work and even think, ideas at the base of computer science.
fact: In the late 1950s engineers invented the microchip, which put many tiny parts onto a small piece of silicon.
fact: Microchips made computers small and cheap enough for homes, cars and phones, and a phone today is millions of times more powerful than the first computers.
fact: From the late 1970s companies such as Apple, IBM and Microsoft made computers and software for homes and offices.
fact: The internet began in 1969 by linking a few computers in American universities and grew into a worldwide network.
fact: In 1989 Tim Berners-Lee, a British scientist at CERN, invented the World Wide Web of pages and links, and it was given to the world for free.
fact: The first mobile phones in the 1980s were as big as bricks, and from 2007 smartphones with touch screens began to spread.
fact: Pong, from 1972, was one of the first popular video games, and later came history games like Civilization and Europa Universalis, which simplify and sometimes change real history.
fact: The internet brought video calls, online shops and encyclopedias, but also scams and false news, so checking sources is an important skill.
exercise: Who wrote what many call the first computer program? | Ada Lovelace | Tim Berners-Lee | Thomas Edison | Florence Nightingale
exercise: For whose machine did Ada Lovelace write her program? | Charles Babbage's | Thomas Edison's | James Watt's | Bill Gates's
exercise: What did Ada Lovelace imagine computers might one day do? | Make music and more than just numbers | Fly to the Moon by themselves | Replace all teachers in 1850 | Nothing beyond adding up
exercise: How big were the first electronic computers? | They filled whole rooms | They fitted in a pocket | They were the size of a coin | They were as small as a watch
exercise: What were early computers used for? | Breaking codes and doing huge calculations | Playing video games with friends after school in the evenings | Watching films | Sending emails to friends
exercise: Where did Alan Turing help break German codes? | Bletchley Park | The Globe theatre | CERN in Switzerland | Ellis Island in New York
exercise: What did the microchip do? | Put many tiny parts onto a small piece of silicon | Made computers much bigger, so they filled whole buildings | Replaced electricity | Invented the internet
exercise: Why could computers come into homes? | Microchips made them small and cheap | Houses were built much bigger, so the giant machines could fit inside | The law ordered it | They became steam-powered
exercise: Which companies helped bring computers into homes and offices? | Apple, IBM and Microsoft | Ford, Benz and Boeing | Nokia, Lego and Ikea | None, homes had no computers
exercise: When did the internet begin? | 1969 | 1492 | 1999 | 2007
exercise: Who invented the World Wide Web? | Tim Berners-Lee | Ada Lovelace | Alexander Graham Bell | Steve Jobs alone
exercise: Where did Tim Berners-Lee invent the Web? | At CERN | At Bletchley Park | At NASA in Florida | At Oxford in 1066
exercise: How much did Tim Berners-Lee charge the world for the Web? | Nothing, it was free | A pound per page | A million dollars | A penny per click
exercise: What were the first mobile phones like? | As big as bricks | As thin as paper | Smaller than a coin | Made of glass only
exercise: When did smartphones with touch screens begin to spread? | From 2007 | From 1957 | From 1907 | From 1977
exercise: What was Pong? | One of the first popular video games | A Chinese computer from the 1950s | The very first website on the World Wide Web | A robot
exercise: Why is checking sources important online? | There are scams and false news | Everything online is always true and checked by experts | Computers ask you to | Websites charge for it
test: my brother says the internet and the web are the same thing. are they? | No, the internet is the network; the web is pages and links on it | Yes, they are exactly the same thing with two different names | No, the web came first in 1969 | No, the internet is only for phones
test: In EU5 your country rules half the world by 1500. What should you remember? | History games simplify and sometimes change real history | The game is a perfect copy of everything that really happened in 1500 | Real history copies the game | Games are always wrong about everything
test: Why is it surprising that Ada Lovelace wrote a program in the 1840s? | The machine was never finished and electronic computers came a century later | She had a smartphone | Programs were common then, and every rich family in London had a computer at home | She lived in 2007
test: If Tim Berners-Lee had charged money for every website, what would probably have happened? | Far fewer people would have made websites | Everyone would have used the web a lot more than they do today | Nothing would change | The internet would go faster
test: A video says a famous person said something shocking, but you find no trusted source. What do you do? | Treat it as maybe false until you can check it | Share it with everyone at once, because important news must spread fast | Believe it because it is a video | Report it as true to the news
test: grandad's first phone was literally a brick?? | Yes, the first mobile phones in the 1980s were brick-sized | No, phones were always tiny | Yes, they were made from a real house brick with buttons stuck on | No, mobile phones came in 1850
test: Why did the war make governments want fast computers? | To break enemy codes and do big calculations quickly | To play games | To send holiday photos to soldiers' families back home | To print more money
test: Your phone is millions of times faster than a 1940s computer. What made that possible? | The microchip | Better steam engines | Building much bigger rooms | Many more light bulbs
test: Why might someone say Alan Turing is a father of computer science? | His ideas about how computers work are its base | He built the very first iPhone in his garden shed | He invented the web | He founded Microsoft
test: A friend says Pong had amazing 3D graphics. What is right? | It was simple dots and lines | It looked like a modern film | It had real actors filmed in colour | It was all in virtual reality goggles
exam: Identify the individual credited by many with the composition of the first computer program. | Ada Lovelace | Alan Mathison Turing | Sir Tim Berners-Lee | Charles Robert Darwin
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between the internet and the World Wide Web? | The internet is the network; the Web is a system of linked pages upon it | They are identical in every respect | The Web preceded the internet by some twenty years and gave rise to it | The internet is confined to telephones
exam: Ascertain the principal consequence of the invention of the microchip. | Computers became small and inexpensive enough for domestic use | Computers came to occupy entire buildings and required many operators | Electricity was rendered unnecessary | The telephone was abolished
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the terms upon which the World Wide Web was made available. | It was made available to the world without charge | It was made available solely to governments | It was sold to a single company for a large sum of money | It was restricted to scientists at CERN
